# Code generated by Claude
"""Replays a recorded run (written by src/run_record.py): checks that the states match the live run exactly and renders its cameras offline.

Typical use:

    from src.replay import replay_run
    report = replay_run("data/runs/run1", out_dir="data/runs/run1/replay", cameras=["eagle_cam"], hz=15)

The replay rebuilds the model from the recording's recipe (and checks its SHA-256 hash, the Secure Hash Algorithm with a 256-bit
digest), then runs src/control_loop.run_loop with cfg["replay"]: the same spawn and settle, the same physics step, but the
recorded control vector (ctrl), base velocity and gripper force limits instead of live command sources. The state after the settle
and the state hash after every control tick must equal the live run's, bit for bit. The first mismatch is reported with its tick and
the parts of the state that differ; the replay stops at the next 1 s state checkpoint (immediately, when the mismatching tick is
itself a checkpoint). Bit-exact reproduction is only guaranteed on the same machine, MuJoCo version and central processing unit
(CPU) architecture; on another one the replay adds a warning to the report and still reports any mismatch.

Cameras: every `hz` (30 by default; it must be a whole divisor of the physics rate) all selected cameras (default: all recorded)
are rendered from the state at that physics step, with the policy cameras' own settings by default (each camera's "shadows" flag
as in robot_specs.CAMERA_SPECS). The argument `video` says what is written:
    "none"      verify only: no rendering, no OpenGL (graphics library) context, no output files (faster)
    "preview"   lossy MP4 (MPEG-4 Part 14 file, OpenCV, MPEG-4 Part 2 video), one video per camera, out_dir/<camera>.mp4, playing at `hz` frames per second of
                sim time
    "training"  lossless Portable Network Graphics (PNG) frames, one folder per camera, out_dir/<camera>/<tick:06d>.png (the control
                tick the frame was rendered in)
    "viewer"    one MP4 of the live viewer's picture (src/viewer_composite.py: the follow camera, the status panel, the three camera
                tiles), 1920x1080 by default, shadows on everywhere, out_dir/<name>.mp4, plus a PNG of the same picture at the
                handoff frame, out_dir/<name>_handoff.png
In the "preview" and "training" modes the run also writes out_dir/frames.csv (comma-separated values with the columns frame, tick,
sim_time) and out_dir/replay_report.json; in the "viewer" mode these files are named out_dir/<name>_frames.csv and
out_dir/<name>_report.json.
"""

